    crm:P183i_has_representation <https://ids.lib.harvard.edu/ids/iiif/493312308/full/full/0/default.jpg> ;
    rdf:value """evening we packed, and had a call from Mr. Pearce and Mr. Sioussat. A Miss Scott called on me in the morning, the only contented woman I have seen in Northampton. She wanted to talk of Gertrude Burton, and to tell me how Einstein had plagiarized her work, wherein I gave her most “adequate” sympathy.
Monday Nov. 2. 1903. New Haven House. New Haven
Came here. The Berensons began to get on my nerves a little bit, and B.B. saw it and was as nice as possible. He said he dreaded it for himself as well, as he  can’t  do more than so much for them.
We rushed at once to the Jarves Collection, and found a nice little roomful of pictures, some really good - a small gallery about on a level with Pisa or Arezzo. The two art professors, Wier and Niemeyer,"""^^xsd:string ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:index 38 ;
    mbdiaries-ontology:part_of <https://mbdiaries.itatti.harvard.edu/resource/diary/1903-04> .

